WEBVTT

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:17.660
<v Ira Glass>Paul's a cop in New York-- Paul Bacon. Officer Bacon. No jokes, please. And one night he did his regular 4:00 to midnight shift, and then he was told that they needed him to cover a second shift in another part of the city. In a little security booth. In an area they were protecting from terrorists, basically.
<v Ira Glass>保罗是纽约的一名警察——保罗·培根。培根警官。请别开玩笑。一天晚上，他像往常一样值了从凌晨4点到午夜的班，然后被告知需要他在城市的另一个地方再值一班。在一个小的保安亭里。基本上是在一个他们保护免受恐怖分子袭击的区域。

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:40.390
<v Ira Glass>At 2:00 AM the other officer who's working in this booth with him takes a lunch break. Goes and takes a nap. And then at 3:00 AM it's Paul's turn for a break. He's exhausted. Remember, he had already worked a full shift as a patrolman. And he decides that he's also going to take a nap. So he gets into his patrol car. And he drives it out into this parking lot a little ways away from the security booth.
<v Ira Glass>凌晨2点，和他一起在岗亭工作的另一名警察去吃午饭了。去打了个盹。凌晨3点轮到保罗休息了。他精疲力竭。记住，他已经作为巡警值满了一个整班。他决定他也去打个盹。于是他坐进巡逻车，开到了离保安亭不远的一个停车场。

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:50.740
<v Paul Bacon>I parked my car in the one available spot thinking, no, this is a parking lot. Nobody's going to be coming by here. It's going to be fine. And so I turned off the ignition and just sat there.
<v Paul Bacon>我把车停在唯一一个空位上，心想，不，这是一个停车场。不会有人经过这里的。应该没问题。所以我关掉点火开关，就坐在那儿。

00:00:50.740 --> 00:01:07.900
<v Paul Bacon>And I realized I can't-- I don't think I'm going to be able to fall asleep sitting up. So I got out of the driver's seat, got in back, and closed the door. And I was asleep within seconds.
<v Paul Bacon>我意识到我不能——我觉得我坐着是没法睡着的。所以我离开驾驶座，坐到后座，关上车门。几秒钟内我就睡着了。

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:17.350
<v Paul Bacon>I had set my alarm watch to go off so that I would get up in plenty of time to go back on post so my partner wouldn't worry where I was. And everything would be fine. It would be smooth.
<v Paul Bacon>我把闹钟手表定好，这样我就有足够的时间回去上岗，免得我的搭档担心我在哪儿。一切都会好起来的。会很顺利的。

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:33.970
<v Paul Bacon>But I woke up a few seconds before my alarm with this terrible realization, which is that I was locked in the back. I just-- I was like-- [GASPING] I'm trapped. That was my first thought. Because you don't get out of the back of a police car. The back of the police car is for prisoners.
<v Paul Bacon>但我比闹钟提前几秒钟醒来，心中有一种可怕的认识，那就是我被锁在后座了。我只是——我想——[喘气]我被困住了。这是我的第一个念头。因为你不能从警车后座出来。警车的后座是给囚犯坐的。

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:39.040
<v Ira Glass>And you know, I never really thought about it, that a police car is really just like a rolling jail cell.
<v Ira Glass>你知道，我从没想过，警车实际上就像一个流动的监狱牢房。

00:01:39.040 --> 00:02:11.180
<v Paul Bacon>That's exactly right. And also, it's hermetically sealed. And remember, it was very cold that night. It was February. And I had closed the grating to the front. And the windows were up, so it was really-- you could just feel there was less oxygen in the air. And also, all the windows were all fogged up, so I could barely see out. And I started to panic.
<v Paul Bacon>完全正确。而且，它是完全密封的。记住，那天晚上很冷。那是二月。我关上了前排的格栅。车窗关着，所以真的——你能感觉到空气中的氧气变少了。而且，所有的窗户都起雾了，所以我几乎看不见外面。我开始恐慌。

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:24.680
<v Paul Bacon>I reached for both of the door handles. Neither of them worked. And so I rolled over on my side, and I pounded against the door as hard as I could with both my feet. And there's just no way out of this car.
<v Paul Bacon>我伸手去拉两个车门把手。都没用。所以我侧身翻滚，用双脚尽我所能地猛踢车门。但这辆车根本出不去。

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:38.240
<v Ira Glass>It's dark. It's cold. But he's only 60 feet away from the security booth where his partner is. And he's right next to a sidewalk. All he needs is for somebody to see him, come over, and lift the door handle from the outside.
<v Ira Glass>天黑了。很冷。但他离他搭档所在的保安亭只有60英尺远。他就靠在人行道旁边。他只需要有人看见他，走过来，从外面拉开车门把手。

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:57.240
<v Ira Glass>And he gets lucky. A Pepsi truck parks right on the street right in front of him. He waves frantically at the driver. He pounds on the glass. He can see the driver peering in at him. And then the driver flees. He flees. Who is going to help some maniac trapped in the back of a police car?
<v Ira Glass>他很幸运。一辆百事可乐卡车停在前面的街上。他疯狂地向司机挥手。他猛敲玻璃。他能看见司机正盯着他看。然后司机逃跑了。他跑了。谁会去帮一个被困在警车后座的疯子呢？

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:29.590
<v Paul Bacon>My next thought was, well, maybe somebody else will come by. And so I wrote-- in the steam on the window I wrote the word "help." And I did it backwards. But then I got to thinking, well, that's not going to be any more convincing. So I wrote-- underneath help I wrote, "I'm a cop." But unfortunately, nobody came by to read my message.
<v Paul Bacon>我下一个念头是，好吧，也许会有别人经过。所以我写——在窗户的蒸汽上我写了“救命”两个字。我写反了。但后来我开始想，好吧，这不会有任何说服力。所以我写——在“救命”下面我写了，“我是警察。”但不幸的是，没有人经过来读我的信息。

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:50.540
<v Ira Glass>His police radio's in the front seat, out of reach. And 3:00 in the morning, exhausted, panicked, it takes him 25 minutes to remember that he is carrying a cell phone-- a cell phone he says he almost never uses. But here's the problem. He's working with an officer that he's never met before in a place he's never been before.
<v Ira Glass>他的警用对讲机在前座，够不到。凌晨3点，精疲力竭，恐慌之中，他花了25分钟才想起自己带着手机——一部他自称几乎从不使用的手机。但问题在于，他和一位从未谋面的警察一起在从未去过的地方工作。

00:03:50.540 --> 00:04:05.460
<v Ira Glass>He has no idea what the phone number is to that little booth just 60 feet away from his parked car-- that little booth where right now, his partner is expecting him back on post, wondering where he is. His first great idea of the night was to take a nap. Now he had a second great idea.
<v Ira Glass>他根本不知道离他停车的地方只有60英尺远的小亭子的电话号码——那个小亭子，他搭档现在正期待他回去上岗，纳闷他在哪里。他那天晚上的第一个好主意是去打个盹。现在他有了第二个好主意。

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:24.340
<v Paul Bacon>I did what I thought I would never have to do ever in my life. And that is to call 911 as a cop. When the operator came on very shortly, she says, well, don't worry. We'll put this through to your central dispatcher.
<v Paul Bacon>我做了我想这辈子永远都不必做的事。那就是以警察的身份拨打911。接线员很快上线，她说，好吧，别担心。我们会把你转接到你的中央调度员那里。

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:43.760
<v Paul Bacon>And she was about to get off the phone with me. And I said, well, wait. There's this very, very important thing to tell the dispatcher. And that is that this needs to go over the radio as a non-emergency. Because any time that a cop requests help and it's not qualified in any way, it sends out the cavalry.
<v Paul Bacon>她正要挂断我的电话。我说，等等。有一件非常、非常重要的事情要告诉调度员。那就是这需要通过无线电作为非紧急事件发送。因为任何时候警察请求帮助，如果没有任何限定，它就会派出大部队。

00:04:43.760 --> 00:05:11.970
<v Paul Bacon>Everybody drops everything they're doing and comes very quickly to that location. And that's a dangerous situation, and all because I made this stupid mistake. So I said, please, make sure that you specify it's a non-emergency. I swear, within a minute, lights and sirens coming from all directions.
<v Paul Bacon>每个人都会放下手中的工作，非常迅速地赶到那个地点。那是一个危险的情况，而这一切仅仅因为我犯了这个愚蠢的错误。所以我说，请务必说明是非紧急情况。我发誓，不到一分钟，从四面八方传来了警灯和警笛声。

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:35.150
<v Ira Glass>Now, part of the chaos is coming from the fact that Paul wasn't really exactly sure where he was. Remember, this is not his regular precinct, so cops are zooming around, just trying to find him. This seems like way too much commotion even for that. Something has gone very, very wrong. So Paul dials 911 again. They tell him that his call had gone out as a 1013. 1013. Officer down.
<v Ira Glass>现在，混乱的部分原因在于保罗并不确定他到底在哪里。记住，这不是他平时的辖区，所以警察们四处狂奔，只是试图找到他。即使考虑到这一点，这也显得太小题大作了。一定是出了什么非常、非常糟糕的问题。所以保罗再次拨打了911。他们告诉他，他的呼叫被作为1013发出了。1013。警官倒地。

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:51.300
<v Paul Bacon>Like, as if a cop was being beaten, or had just been shot. And that's why everybody was going all over the place. My call had basically woken up the entire midnight squad. Probably half that squad was also sleeping, and now I had gotten everybody's blood going.
<v Paul Bacon>就像有警察正在被殴打，或者刚刚中枪一样。这就是为什么每个人都到处乱跑。我的呼叫基本上惊醒了整个午夜小队。可能那个小队的一半人也在睡觉，现在我把所有人的血性都调动起来了。

00:05:51.300 --> 00:06:13.870
<v Paul Bacon>So my partner up in the booth finally got up and started walking down into the parking lot. And I saw him coming. And I took my flashlight off my belt. And I'm shaking my flashlight like crazy, just to show him that this is not a headlight of some car. This is a person being very creative, and very emphatic about letting you know that this is somebody stuck.
<v Paul Bacon>所以我在岗亭里的搭档终于起来，开始往停车场走。我看见他来了。我从腰带上取下我的手电筒。我疯狂地摇晃着我的手电筒，只是为了让他看到这不是什么汽车的前灯。这是一个非常具有创造性，并且非常强调让你知道这是有人被困住的人。

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:48.640
<v Paul Bacon>And he sees me from about 50 yards away. And he takes his flashlight off and jingles it back at me, and then starts to walk away. He's just thinking I'm having a good time, you know? Like, ha ha ha. He was walking away. So I had to rap really hard on my window again. And I rapped so hard that I cut my knuckles, but he got the point. And he came down to my car, just lifted the door handle. It took him two seconds to let me out.
<v Paul Bacon>他在大约50码外看见了我。他取下他的手电筒，对我晃了晃，然后开始走开。他只是以为我在玩得很开心，你知道吗？就像，哈哈。他走开了。所以我不得不再次用力敲击我的窗户。我敲得太用力，把指关节都割破了，但他明白了。他走到我的车前，只是拉开了车门把手。他只用了两秒钟就把我放了出来。

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:56.430
<v Ira Glass>So he's free, and they call off the cavalry. Nobody in his regular precinct ever found out about it.
<v Ira Glass>所以他自由了，他们取消了大部队。他平时所在的辖区里从来没有人发现这件事。

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:10.970
<v Ira Glass>Today on our radio show we bring you stories like this one. Stories that begin with a bright idea-- like, for instance, you're going to park your car 60 feet from your partner, climb in, and take a nap. And then things happen. More than you bargained for, more than you wanted.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的电台节目中，我们带给你像这样的故事。这些故事以一个好主意开始——比如，你打算把车停在离搭档60英尺远的地方，钻进去，打个盹。然后事情就发生了。超过了你预想的，超过了你想要的。

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:27.470
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week we bring you a bunch of stories organized around a single thought. And this week, that thought is "It Seemed Like a Good Idea at the Time." Our show in four acts today, each of which starts off with great hope and a shiny new idea.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们带给你一系列围绕一个思想组织的各种故事。而这一周，这个思想是“当时看来是个好主意”。我们今天节目的四幕，每一幕都以巨大的希望和一个闪亮的新点子开始。

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:51.640
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Luck of the Irish. In that act, a bunch of people become very convinced that with one simple purchase, they can change their lives forever. Act Two, Taxation Without Inebriation. In that act, how to make a decision to hire somebody who absolutely, positively is going to do you no good-- and you can tell they're going to do you no good-- and why you may never fire that person.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，爱尔兰人的运气。在那一幕里，一群人变得非常确信，只要进行一次简单的购买，他们就能永远改变他们的生活。第二幕，无醉酒的征税。在那一幕里，如何做出决定去雇佣一个绝对、肯定对你没有任何好处的人——而且你能看出他们对你没有任何好处——以及为什么你可能永远不会解雇那个人。

00:07:51.640 --> 00:08:18.730
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Bad Morning America. Davy Rothbart goes onto morning television and does a little experiment on his hosts. Act Four, The Function of the Heart. An 11-year-old girl comes up with a simple scheme on how she can finally get to really know her dad, her dad who is not so keen on being known. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，坏的美国早晨。戴维·罗斯巴特上了早间电视节目，对他的主持人们做了一个小实验。第四幕，心脏的功能。一个11岁的小女孩想出了一个简单的计划，关于她如何最终能真正了解她的爸爸，那个不太热衷于被了解的爸爸。请留下来。

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:28.900
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Luck of the Irish. This first story takes place far from the New York police force. This is a very different kind of job, maybe about as different as you can get.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，爱尔兰人的运气。这第一个故事发生在远离纽约警察部队的地方。这是一种非常不同的工作，也许是你能想到的最不同的工作。

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:35.570
<v Katie Else>Well, I was in Riverdance. But I don't actually dance. I just sing. There's a lead female singer.
<v Katie Else>嗯，我在《大河之舞》里。但我其实并不跳舞。我只是唱歌。有一位女领唱。

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:40.870
<v Ira Glass>It must be a terrible thing to be the singer in Riverdance because nobody actually-- it's all about the dancing, right?
<v Ira Glass>做《大河之舞》的歌手一定很糟糕，因为没人真的——全都是关于跳舞的，对吧？

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:47.120
<v Katie Else>No, exactly. And every now and then, someone will say something like, it's called Riverdance, not Riversing.
<v Katie Else>不，完全正确。偶尔会有人说些什么，这叫《大河之舞》，不是《大河之唱》。

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:56.880
<v Ira Glass>Mr. And Mrs. America, please meet Katie Else. Katie was touring with Riverdance, doing eight shows a week, working every day. Any day that they weren't on stage, they would be traveling.
<v Ira Glass>美国先生和女士们，请认识一下凯蒂·埃尔斯。凯蒂正在随《大河之舞》巡演，每周表演八场，每天都在工作。任何他们不在舞台上的日子，他们都会在路上。

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:15.350
<v Ira Glass>70 people in the company. That's four singers, a band, assorted tech guys and support people, and 40-- yes, 40-- dancers doing that Irish stepdancing thing they do. I don't even know if it's called stepdancing. You know what I'm talking about here? The Michael Flatley-- of course you do, because you've seen television.
<v Ira Glass>剧团里有70个人。那是四个歌手，一个乐队，各种技术人员和支持人员，还有40个——是的，40个——跳那种他们跳的爱尔兰踢踏舞的舞者。我甚至不知道那是不是叫踢踏舞。你知道我在说什么吧？迈克尔·弗莱利——当然你知道，因为你看过电视。

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:27.020
<v Ira Glass>Anyway, two months into the tour, a time when working every day, they're getting kind of tired with the monotony of working every day. And they needed some excitement. And this idea spread through the company like wildfire.
<v Ira Glass>总之，巡演两个月后，在这个每天都在工作的时间里，他们对每天工作的单调感到有些厌倦了。他们需要一些兴奋。而这个想法像野火一样在剧团里蔓延开来。

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:42.970
<v Katie Else>I think it was the idea of the lead dancer and dance captain. He has this ridiculous luck with raffles. We have a raffle every Friday, and he has won it like five times. And he just has ridiculous luck with this.
<v Katie Else>我想是领舞和舞蹈队长的主意。他在抽奖方面有这种荒谬的运气。我们每周五都有抽奖，他已经赢了大概五次了。他在这方面运气好得离谱。

00:09:42.970 --> 00:10:01.470
<v Katie Else>And he just happened to notice, on the internet I guess, that Mega-Millions had gotten up to like $360 million or something like that. So he suggested that we all do a syndicate ticket. It's like you pool your money. So $5 gets five tickets. But everybody has a share in all the tickets.
<v Katie Else>他刚好注意到，我想是在网上，超级百万彩票的奖金已经到了3.6亿美元左右。所以他建议我们大家都买一张联合彩票。就像你汇集你的钱。所以5美元可以买五张票。但每个人都分享所有票的份额。

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:21.730
<v Ira Glass>Not to complicate it, right? They're playing the lottery. So the company's physical therapist, who's a guy named Scott, he is the one who organized the whole thing and collected everybody's money. And the reason why it's him is-- OK, if you picture this company of 40 dancers, it's like-- and I hate to bring up this analogy, but honestly it's very, very apt-- the Starship Enterprise.
<v Ira Glass>不把事情复杂化，对吧？他们在买彩票。所以剧团的物理治疗师，一个叫斯科特的人，就是组织整个事情并收集每个人钱的人。之所以是他，是因为——好吧，如果你想象一下这40名舞者的剧团，它就像——而且我讨厌提起这个比喻，但老实说它非常、非常贴切——进取号星舰。

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:45.520
<v Ira Glass>Remember this? I always thought that it was really a little weird that the one job besides actually flying around and exploring space, the one set that they bothered to build on this television show that wasn't actually adventure related, was the doctor's office. I mean of all the things, right? And basically, it's like that with Riverdance. Scott, the physical therapist, has a very big role behind the scenes in Riverdance.
<v Ira Glass>记得这个吗？我一直觉得很奇怪，除了真的在太空飞来飞去探索之外，他们在这个电视节目里费心搭建的唯一不是关于冒险的场景，竟然是医生办公室。我是说在所有事物中，对吧？基本上，《大河之舞》也一样。物理治疗师斯科特在《大河之舞》的幕后扮演着非常重要的角色。

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:51.970
<v Katie Else>Well, his office is like a center for gossip, and people gather there during the show.
<v Katie Else>嗯，他的办公室就像一个八卦中心，人们在演出期间聚集在那里。

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:54.700
<v Ira Glass>And how'd the pitch go? Give me the pitch.
<v Ira Glass>那么这个推销是怎么进行的？把推销给我。

00:10:54.700 --> 00:11:08.830
<v Katie Else>Well, he didn't-- it was just basically like, everybody's doing this. We want everybody to get involved. Because then our chances will be better, which is kind of a ridiculous thing to say when you're talking about the lottery because your chances are so slim. To be like, one more person.
<v Katie Else>嗯，他没有——基本上就像是，大家都在做这个。我们希望每个人都参与进来。因为那样我们的机会会更好，这在谈论彩票时是一种相当荒谬的说法，因为你的机会太渺茫了。就像说，多一个人。

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:27.610
<v Katie Else>But we figured out the stats. I guess the stats on the Mega-Millions at that point was 1 to 760 trillion. But we figured we had like a 340 to 760 trillion chance, which sounded better than one.
<v Katie Else>但我们算出了统计数据。我想当时超级百万彩票的统计数据是1比760万亿。但我们算了一下，我们大概有340比760万亿的机会，这听起来比1要好。

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:29.510
<v Ira Glass>Because you guys would buy 340 tickets.
<v Ira Glass>因为你们会买340张彩票。

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:30.660
<v Katie Else>Yeah.
<v Katie Else>是的。

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:46.450
<v Ira Glass>But did it seem in some way reasonable? I'm sitting here, actually. And you're saying these numbers. And I'm writing out on a piece of paper 760. And I realize like, I don't even know-- wait. Is trillion the next one up from billion? And then I'm writing 340.
