00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:33.750
<v Katie Else>And pretty much everybody was there. We're at a bunch of different tables. And every table has a different computer printout to read over the lottery numbers. So we get the numbers. And we're just poring over these sheets. And we're just getting nothing, nothing, not even anything remotely close.
<v Katie Else>基本上每个人都在那儿。我们在不同的桌子。每一张桌子都有不同的电脑打印输出阅读彩票号码。所以我们拿到了号码。我们只是在仔细阅读这些纸张。我们什么都没得到，什么都没有，甚至没有什么接近的。

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:35.460
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:43.170
<v Katie Else>So then someone's like, I want to see the originals. What if you made a mistake in the computer? So we're going through the originals and collectively--
<v Katie Else>所以然后有人说，我想看原件。如果你在电脑里犯错了呢？所以我们正在检查原件，集体地——

00:21:43.170 --> 00:21:48.420
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. I love the instinct of that, though. The instinct is, this must be wrong. I mean clearly we won.
<v Ira Glass>是的。我喜欢那种直觉。直觉是，这肯定是错的。我的意思是显然我们赢了。

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:48.990
<v Katie Else>Exactly.
<v Katie Else>完全正确。

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:50.880
<v Ira Glass>There's been some sort of mistake here.
<v Ira Glass>这里肯定有什么错误。

00:21:50.880 --> 00:22:10.440
<v Katie Else>He made a mistake. But it wasn't even like there was one that was close. And someone actually brought up-- someone actually posed the idea that maybe Scott had put the numbers in wrong, and we had won the lottery. He had won the lottery and was going to take all the money.
<v Katie Else>他犯了个错误。但甚至没有一个是接近的。有人甚至提出来——有人实际上提出了这个想法，也许斯科特把号码输入错了，我们已经赢了彩票。他赢了彩票，打算把钱全拿走。

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:11.160
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:21.190
<v Katie Else>It's amazing how worked up you can get yourself, and especially-- it's like gang mentality-- how worked up you can get each other. And then you just crash.
<v Katie Else>你能让自己变得多兴奋真是太不可思议了，尤其是——就像黑帮心理——你们能互相把对方煽动到什么程度。然后你只是崩溃。

00:22:21.190 --> 00:22:31.020
<v Ira Glass>Nobody even hung around, she said. They all went straight to bed. And the next day, they did a lousy show, incredibly low energy.
<v Ira Glass>她说，没人留下来。他们都直接去睡觉了。第二天，他们做了一场烂透了的演出，能量极低。

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:48.030
<v Ira Glass>And here's the disturbing thing. The audience loved it. Couldn't tell at all, which either means that A, they are such solid performers and this material, the Riverdance material, is so solid that even on their worst day, they are pretty damn good.
<v Ira Glass>这是令人不安的事情。观众很喜欢。完全看不出来，这意味着要么A，他们是如此可靠的表演者，而这部材料，《大河之舞》的材料，是如此稳固，以至于即使在他们最糟糕的一天，他们也相当棒。

00:22:48.030 --> 00:23:06.630
<v Ira Glass>Or B-- and this is kind of ugly to say, but I'm just going to say it. When you and I and a lot of people, when we get together in a mass group, and we get together in an audience, and we're sitting in the theater in an audience, we just get stupid. And it doesn't matter if the performers try. Katie actually sees it somewhere in between those two.
<v Ira Glass>或者B——这话说出来有点丑陋，但我还是会说出来。当你和我以及很多人，当我们聚在一起成一个大群体，当我们聚在一起成为观众，我们坐在剧院里成为观众，我们只是变傻了。而表演者是否努力并不重要。凯蒂实际上看到了介于两者之间的地方。

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:17.560
<v Katie Else>They're going to see kind of what they want to see. They came wanting to see this thing. They think you're giving them all this energy and this amazing performance. And that's what they're going to see.
<v Katie Else>他们去看某种他们想看的东西。他们来是因为想要看到这个东西。他们认为你正在给他们所有的这些能量和这场精彩的表演。那就是他们要看的。

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:32.680
<v Ira Glass>Which in a way is just people being nice, assuming the performers are doing their best onstage. You know? We want to laugh at the comedian's jokes. We want them to be funny. It's a nice thing. This whole experience raised some very basic questions about performing.
<v Ira Glass>这在某种程度上只是人们表现得友善，假设表演者在台上尽力而为。你知道吗？我们想对喜剧演员的笑话发笑。我们想让他们很有趣。这是一件好事。整个经历引发了一些关于表演的非常基本的问题。

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:56.420
<v Katie Else>And in theory, I guess-- we were talking. We were like, you know, I guess that's how much energy you're supposed to give the audience, ideally, every night. But you can't. Like I couldn't do-- after we lost the lottery, obviously, I turned to one of my friends. And I was like, I don't think I could do another lotto show. I don't think it would be possible. It's just too draining.
<v Katie Else>在理论上，我想——我们在交谈。我们就像，你知道，我想这就是你理想中每晚应该给观众的能量。但你不能。比如我不能做——在我们输掉彩票后，显然，我转向我的一个朋友。我就说，我不认为我能再做一场彩票秀。我不认为那是可能的。这只是太消耗了。

00:23:56.420 --> 00:24:01.450
<v Ira Glass>Because I would think it's more fun to perform if you're more hyped up. You know?
<v Ira Glass>因为我认为如果你更兴奋，表演会更有趣。你知道吗？

00:24:01.450 --> 00:24:27.020
<v Katie Else>It definitely is more fun. It kind of puts performing in perspective because-- I don't know. Maybe some people just get themselves really hyped up and can do that every night. And good for them. But eight shows a week, that's a lot of shows. I don't think I could.
<v Katie Else>这绝对更有趣。这让表演更有视角感，因为——我不知道。也许有些人只是让自己变得非常兴奋并且能够每晚都那样做。为他们高兴。但每周八场演出，那可是很多演出。我不认为我能。

00:24:27.020 --> 00:24:42.120
<v Ira Glass>One of the conclusions that you all could come to is that, although you were dancing your hardest and trying to direct that energy towards the Powerball, perhaps that isn't really what determines how the Powerball falls.
<v Ira Glass>你们所有人可能得出的结论之一是，尽管你们在最卖力地跳舞并试图把那份能量指向强力球，也许那并不是真正决定强力球如何掉落的东西。

00:24:42.120 --> 00:24:44.210
<v Katie Else>Perhaps.
<v Katie Else>也许。

00:24:44.210 --> 00:24:49.140
<v Ira Glass>Well, or possibly you weren't dancing quite hard enough.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，或者可能你跳得不够卖力。

00:24:49.140 --> 00:24:51.260
<v Katie Else>Yeah.
<v Katie Else>是的。

00:24:51.260 --> 00:24:56.760
<v Ira Glass>Or there was a touring company of Chicago that really danced.
<v Ira Glass>或者有一个芝加哥的巡演剧团真的跳了舞。

00:24:56.760 --> 00:25:13.430
<v Katie Else>Well, I think the people who ended up winning was-- it was another syndicate ticket, but it was nurses. So I don't know what they did. They put as much energy into changing bedpans and putting in IVs as they could.
<v Katie Else>嗯，我认为最终赢了的人是——那是另一张联合彩票，但那是护士。所以我不知道她们做了什么。她们把尽可能多的能量投入到了换便盆和输液中。

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:14.790
<v Ira Glass>Or saving lives.
<v Ira Glass>或者拯救生命。

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:18.230
<v Katie Else>Or saving lives.
<v Katie Else>或者拯救生命。

00:25:18.230 --> 00:25:20.250
<v Ira Glass>One little dance troupe can't compete with that, man.
<v Ira Glass>一个小舞团竞争不过那个，老兄。

00:25:20.250 --> 00:25:20.730
<v Katie Else>No.
<v Katie Else>不。

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:24.450
<v Ira Glass>They get in there, and they save a couple lives for the lottery. That's some energy.
<v Ira Glass>她们进去，她们为彩票拯救了几条生命。那是能量。

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:34.170
<v Katie Else>That's true. I bet they saved a life. Damn it.
<v Katie Else>那是真的。我打赌她们救了一条命。该死。

00:25:34.170 --> 00:25:47.270
<v Ira Glass>Singer Katie Else. Since doing this interview, she's quit Riverdance. She has an album coming out. Info at katieelse.com.
<v Ira Glass>歌手凯蒂·埃尔斯。自从做完这次采访，她已经退出了《大河之舞》。她有一张专辑要发行。信息在katieelse.com。

00:25:47.270 --> 00:26:30.450
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "LOTTERY" BY MARKY RAMONE AND THE INTRUDERS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - "LOTTERY" BY MARKY RAMONE AND THE INTRUDERS]

00:26:30.450 --> 00:26:51.160
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Taxation Without Inebriation. So you need help. You're in over your head, and you turn to a professional. Often, that is a good idea. It just depends on who the professional is that you turn to. A couple years ago, Joel Lovell and his wife had financial problems. Tax time was coming around. And for once, he says, they got the bright idea to take appropriate steps.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，无醉酒的征税。所以你需要帮助。你力不从心，你求助于专业人士。通常，那是个好主意。这取决于你求助的是谁。几年前，乔尔·洛维尔和他的妻子有经济问题。报税时间快到了。他说，他们难得有了采取适当措施的好主意。

00:26:51.160 --> 00:27:04.310
<v Joel Lovell>The first time we went to see Len, he spent an hour and a half talking about movies and musicals and the actors he'd had as clients way back when nobody knew their names. "F. Murray Abraham owes me," he said. "I've saved his ass for years."
<v Joel Lovell>我们第一次去见伦的时候，他花了一个半小时谈论电影和音乐剧，以及他在没人知道他们名字时曾作为客户的演员们。“F·默里·亚伯拉罕欠我的人情，”他说。“我多年来拯救了他的屁股。”

00:27:04.310 --> 00:27:22.200
<v Joel Lovell>Eventually, my wife Kate cut in and explained that we were pretty worried, that the bulk of our income that year had been untaxed, and that we hadn't paid any quarterly estimates. Kate made most of her living then as an improv actor, and I made most of mine as a freelance writer. And the truth was we'd spent everything we'd earned and more.
<v Joel Lovell>最终，我的妻子凯特插话解释说我们非常担心，那一年的大部分收入都没有纳税，而且我们没有支付任何季度预估税。凯特当时大部分时间是作为即兴演员谋生，而我大部分时间是作为自由撰稿人。事实是我们已经花光了我们赚的所有钱，甚至更多。

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:49.650
<v Joel Lovell>Len looked at us incredulously. "Can one of you explain to me," he said, "how the hell Ordinary People beats out Raging Bull? Does that make a lick of freaking sense?" It's a little hard to explain now. But at the time, it seemed charming rather than worrisome that our accountant was more concerned about what won best picture in 1980 than about our unpaid taxes.
<v Joel Lovell>伦难以置信地看着我们。“你们中谁能向我解释，”他说，“《普通人》到底是怎么打败《愤怒的公牛》的？这他妈的有哪怕一点道理吗？”现在解释起来有点难。但在当时，我们的会计师更关心1980年什么电影赢得了最佳影片，而不是我们未缴纳的税款，这看起来很迷人，而不是令人担忧。

00:27:49.650 --> 00:28:05.160
<v Joel Lovell>On the subway ride from Len's Manhattan apartment back to Brooklyn, I went on and on about how reassuring it was to take our financial mess to a guy like him. His whole attitude was comforting, I kept saying. Even the fact that he was drunk while we sat there in his office, it helped put things in perspective.
<v Joel Lovell>在从伦的曼哈顿公寓回布鲁克林的地铁上，我不停地谈论把我们的财务烂摊子交给像他这样的人是多么令人安心。他整个态度很令人安慰，我不断地说。即使是我们坐在他的办公室里时他喝醉了的事实，也有助于把事情看清楚。

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:26.280
<v Joel Lovell>When we got home, there were three messages on our answering machine, each one more slurred than the one that had come before it. "It's Len," the first message started. "You kids are screwed. I'll do what I can, but this is a horror show. You should know that." Then a beep and, "It's me, Len. I don't know what the hell we're going to do here."
<v Joel Lovell>当我们回到家时，我们的答录机上有三条留言，每一条都比前一条更含糊不清。“我是伦，”第一条留言开始了。“你们这些孩子完蛋了。我会尽我所能，但这简直是个恐怖片。你们应该知道。”然后是一声哔声，然后是，“是我，伦。我不知道我们他妈的要在这里做什么。”

00:28:26.280 --> 00:29:01.400
<v Joel Lovell>And finally this-- which we had to play over a few times to understand. "Kate, you're a comedienne, for Christ's sake. If you go out to drinks with Steve Martin, you can-- you can write that off. That sort of crap, that's legit." Kate, who had never met Steve Martin, stared at the answering machine for some time. Eventually, she looked up at me and said, "We can't ever go to Len again."
<v Joel Lovell>最后是这个——我们不得不重播了几次才能听懂。“凯特，看在上帝份上，你是个喜剧演员。如果你和史蒂夫·马丁出去喝酒，你可以——你可以把那记入费用。那种破事，是合法的。”从未见过史蒂夫·马丁的凯特盯着答录机看了好一会儿。最终，她抬头看着我说，“我们再也不能去找伦了。”

00:29:01.400 --> 00:29:15.310
<v Joel Lovell>The next year, I went to Len again. I know it doesn't make any sense. I knew it even then. But the thing was he saved us a ton of money. And while he did it in a way that was not technically legal, he made it all seem perfectly justified.
<v Joel Lovell>第二年，我又去找了伦。我知道这根本说不通。我当时甚至也知道。但问题是他为我们节省了一大笔钱。虽然他做的方式从技术上讲不合法，但他让这一切看起来完全正当。

00:29:15.310 --> 00:29:30.820
<v Joel Lovell>When I went to pick up our return and paused for a moment over some figures that were utterly fictional, Len scoffed at me. "Look," he said. "They want to screw you. And I'll lie up the "waz" to make sure you don't get screwed. [BLEEP] them. That's how you have to think about these things."
<v Joel Lovell>当我去取我们的报税表，在一些完全虚构的数字上停顿了一会儿时，伦对我嗤之以鼻。“看，”他说。“他们想坑你。为了确保你不被坑，我会胡说八道到极致的。[消音]他们。这就是你必须看待这些事情的方式。”

00:29:30.820 --> 00:30:07.740
<v Joel Lovell>It was more than just the money, though. There was something-- I don't know-- kind of cool about having a tax preparer who couldn't have had less regard for the government's rules. Len did the taxes of artists and writers and actors. And the thing he valued about them had nothing to do with their money. At a time when a few of my friends had become suddenly, inexplicably rich, just sitting in Len's shambles of an office, looking at his albums and books, at the paintings and sculptures he'd accepted from clients in lieu of actual payment, it made me feel like I was a part of this creative community.
<v Joel Lovell>不过，这不仅仅是关于钱。有一些——我不知道——有点酷，拥有一个对政府规定毫不理会的报税员。伦处理艺术家、作家和演员的税。他看重他们的东西与他们的钱无关。当时，我的一些朋友突然变得不可思议地富有，仅仅是坐在伦那乱七八糟的办公室里，看着他的相册和书，看着他从客户那里接受的代替实际支付的绘画和雕塑，这让我觉得我是这个创意社区的一部分。

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:34.040
<v Joel Lovell>It was about 10:00 in the morning when I saw Len, a week before the filing deadline. And he was chain-smoking Parliaments and drinking scotch from a stainless steel tumbler. His desk was covered with manila folders, dozens of them. They were coffee ringed and cigarette stained, his calculations scratched across them in a tiny, illegible hand. There was a drum kit in the corner of the room piled high with even more folders. And next to Len's desk on a metal cart was a computer that looked like it had just come out of the box.
<v Joel Lovell>报税截止日期前一周，我见到伦的时候大约是早上10点。他正连环抽着议会牌香烟，从不锈钢杯里喝着苏格兰威士忌。他的办公桌上满是马尼拉文件夹，几十个。它们上面有咖啡圈和香烟渍，他的计算结果用一种微小、难以辨认的字体写在上面。房间角落里有一套鼓，上面堆着更多的文件夹。伦的桌子旁边是一辆金属小车，上面有一台看起来刚从盒子里拿出来的电脑。

00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:46.020
<v Joel Lovell>"They told me this freaking program would make it easier," Len said. "But I don't know what the hell." He looked exhausted and distracted. "There's this kid they keep sending over to help me out, but everything he says just confuses me more."
<v Joel Lovell>“他们告诉我这个该死的程序会让事情变得更容易，”伦说。“但我不知道他妈的怎么用。”他看起来精疲力竭且心烦意乱。“他们一直派来帮我的那个孩子，但他说的每一句话只会让我更困惑。”

00:30:46.020 --> 00:31:08.920
<v Joel Lovell>He slouched in his chair and rubbed his fists in tight circles around his eyes, then gave his head a little shake. "Ah, crap," he said. "Your old lady's a kick in the pants. She's a funny chick. I'm sorry she's not here." He started to chuckle, but the chuckle turned into a coughing fit. And Len hacked away for a solid half a minute, waving me off with one hand and resting his forehead on the edge of his desk.
<v Joel Lovell>他瘫坐在椅子里，用拳头在眼睛周围紧紧地画圈，然后头稍微摇了摇。“啊，该死，”他说。“你的老太婆很带劲。她是个有趣的妞。我很遗憾她不在这儿。”他开始咯咯笑，但笑声变成了咳嗽。伦咳嗽了整整半分钟，一只手把我挥开，前额靠在桌子边缘。

00:31:08.920 --> 00:31:22.480
<v Joel Lovell>Eventually, a tremendously thin and wrinkled woman appeared in the door. "You OK, Lenny?" she asked. He lifted his head. "Yeah, good," he said. And the blood began to slowly retreat from his face. The woman handed him a handkerchief.
<v Joel Lovell>最终，一个非常瘦弱、满脸皱纹的女人出现在门口。“你还好吗，伦尼？”她问道。他抬起头。“是的，很好，”他说。血液开始慢慢地从他的脸上退去。那个女人递给他一条手帕。

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:37.040
<v Joel Lovell>And Len pulled a wad of crumpled bills out of his breast pocket. "Go buy us some steaks," he said. "Wine too if there's anything left over." Then he looked at me and smiled. "My ex," he said, "she's helping me out of a jam here."
<v Joel Lovell>伦从胸前口袋里掏出一叠皱巴巴的钞票。“去给我们买点牛排，”他说。“如果还剩点钱，买点酒。”然后他看着我笑了。“我的前妻，”他说，“她在这儿帮我摆脱困境。”

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:53.510
<v Joel Lovell>I nodded and held on to my folder filled with another year's bad news. I said something about how great it was that they could still work together, he and his ex, especially under the gun like this. "Yeah, we've been through it," he said. And then he went on to tell me about how the two of them went on this trip to Puerto Rico years ago, shortly after they were married.
<v Joel Lovell>我点点头，紧紧抓着我装满又一年坏消息的文件夹。我说了一些关于他们还能一起工作，他和他的前妻，尤其是在这种紧迫关头，真是太棒了。 “是的，我们已经经历过了，”他说。然后他继续告诉我他们俩在结婚后不久是如何去波多黎各旅行的。

00:31:53.510 --> 00:32:11.600
<v Joel Lovell>"We drank rum drinks and sat around burning our asses off all week. It was great. On the last day, we barely had two nickels to rub together. So this is what we did. We went down to the pool for a few last drinks-- you know, just left all our crap in our room, right? Then caught a cab to the airport and got on the plane with nothing but our towels and sandals."
<v Joel Lovell>“我们喝着朗姆酒饮料，整个星期坐在那儿烧我们的屁股。太棒了。在最后一天，我们几乎连两枚镍币都凑不到一起。所以这就是我们做的。我们下楼到游泳池去喝最后几杯——你知道，只是把我们所有的烂东西留在了房间里，对吧？然后打车去机场，只带着我们的毛巾和凉鞋上了飞机。”

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:43.620
<v Joel Lovell>He started laughing again, hard. And I braced myself for another coughing fit, but it didn't come. "Ah, Christ," he said. "Those were good days. You could do stuff like that back then." I just sat there trying to take in what that meant. 30 years ago, you could check into a hotel in Puerto Rico for a week, then not pay and leave all your belongings in your room, then go to the airport in your swim trunks and fly back to New York City?
<v Joel Lovell>他又开始大笑起来。我做好准备迎接又一次咳嗽，但没来。“啊，上帝，”他说。“那是些好日子。那时你可以做那种事情。”我只是坐在那儿试图领会这意味着什么。30年前，你可以入住波多黎各的一家酒店一个星期，然后不付钱，把你所有的个人物品留在房间里，然后穿着游泳裤去机场飞回纽约市？

00:32:43.620 --> 00:33:05.060
<v Joel Lovell>While I considered this, Len pulled himself upright and surveyed the mess on his desk. He let out a long, dramatic sigh and said, "Ah, crap. There's no use putting it off any longer, right? Let's have the mess." I thought of my wife, the funny chick, back home in Brooklyn. She was really tired of us being jackasses about money, and I couldn't believe that she'd look kindly on this scene.
<v Joel Lovell>当我考虑这一点时，伦坐直了身子，环顾桌子上的乱摊子。他长长地叹了口气，戏剧性地说，“啊，该死。再拖下去也没用，对吧？把烂摊子拿来吧。”我想起了我的妻子，那个有趣的妞，回到布鲁克林的家里。她真的厌倦了我们在钱的问题上像个蠢驴，我不相信她会喜欢这个场景。

00:33:05.060 --> 00:33:21.380
<v Joel Lovell>"I don't know, Len," I said. "It looks like you have your hands full this year. Why don't I take all this to someone else this time?" "Just give me the freaking folder," Len said. I started to make an excuse. But before I could get anything out of my mouth, Len heaved himself out of his chair and fell across the width of his desk.
<v Joel Lovell>“我不知道，伦，”我说。“看起来你今年够忙的了。为什么我不这次把这些带给别人？”“只要把该死的文件夹给我，”伦说。我开始找借口。但在我能从嘴里说出什么之前，伦把自己从椅子上费力地挪起来，扑在桌子上。

00:33:21.380 --> 00:33:44.810
<v Joel Lovell>He grabbed my folder with one of his hands, and I watched his face go crimson. "Come on," he said. "I need this." I let go. Len fell back into his chair with my folder in his hands and closed his eyes. I knew I shouldn't leave it at this. That I needed to stand up, and maybe get a little mean and self-righteous, and tell him to give me my folder, and then walk out of there.
<v Joel Lovell>他用一只手抓住了我的文件夹，我看着他的脸变得通红。“来吧，”他说。“我需要这个。”我放手了。伦手里拿着我的文件夹倒回椅子里，闭上了眼睛。我知道我不应该就此罢手。我需要站起来，也许变得刻薄和自以为是，告诉他把我的文件夹给我，然后离开那儿。

00:33:44.810 --> 00:34:01.790
<v Joel Lovell>But I couldn't. I'd like to believe that it was out of some sort of compassion. That I was looking at a broken man and choosing not to break him further. And maybe that was part of it. But it also had something to do with my own private disgrace at how little money I had and at how badly I managed it.
<v Joel Lovell>但我做不到。我想相信那是出于某种同情心。我看着一个破碎的人，选择不再进一步摧毁他。也许那是其中的一部分。但这也与我对自己有多少钱以及我管理得有多糟糕的私下羞耻有关。

00:34:01.790 --> 00:34:21.240
<v Joel Lovell>It felt to me like it was just a facet of something larger and more troublesome, an inability to take much of anything very seriously. Without Len, I'd have to show that folder to someone else who'd probably look down on me. Len was less my accountant than the guardian of my shame. And in that respect, I still needed his services.
<v Joel Lovell>我觉得这只是更大、更麻烦的事情的一个侧面，即无法非常认真地对待很多事情。没有伦，我就得把那个文件夹拿给别人看，他们可能会瞧不起我。伦与其说是我的会计，不如说是我的羞耻感的守护者。在那方面，我仍然需要他的服务。

00:34:21.240 --> 00:34:43.960
<v Joel Lovell>So I said OK, and walked out and waited for the elevator. When it arrived, Len's ex-wife stepped off with their steaks and wine. We smiled at each other, embarrassed. And she said, he'll be better next year. This one's a toughie.
<v Joel Lovell>所以我说了好，然后走了出去，等着电梯。当它到达时，伦的前妻带着他们的牛排和酒走了出来。我们互相微笑，感到尴尬。她说，他明年会好起来的。这个是一个难题。

00:34:43.960 --> 00:35:01.100
<v Joel Lovell>Len never called us to sign our return. I assumed he'd filed for an extension. And I called him a few times and left messages trying to find out, though I didn't try very hard. Several months later, we received a letter telling us that Len had died in April, days before the filing deadline.
<v Joel Lovell>伦从没打电话让我们签署我们的报税表。我以为他申请了延期。我打过几次电话给他，留言想了解情况，虽然我没怎么认真打。几个月后，我们收到一封信，告诉我们伦在四月份死了，就在报税截止日期前几天。

00:35:01.100 --> 00:35:17.010
<v Joel Lovell>I called the accountant who'd sent the letter. And he told me they had taken over a bunch of Len's clients. Our return had never been filed, he said. My wife and I owed a lot of money. He'd do what he could to explain the circumstances, but there were going to be some fines and penalties to pay. I understood that, right?
<v Joel Lovell>我打电话给发信的那位会计师。他告诉我他们接管了伦的一大堆客户。我们的报税表从未提交过，他说。我和我妻子欠了很多钱。他会尽力解释情况，但会有一些罚款和罚金要付。我理解这一点，对吧？

00:35:17.010 --> 00:35:30.780
<v Joel Lovell>I thanked him, and hung up, and called the IRS. "I know I should have done something," I found myself admitting to a woman who repeatedly insisted that I and no one else was responsible for my taxes. I know I should have taken care of it differently.
<v Joel Lovell>我谢了他，挂断电话，给美国国税局打了电话。“我知道我本应该做点什么的，”我发现自己向一位一再坚持我以及除了我之外没有人对我的税负责的女士承认道。我知道我本应该用不同的方式处理它。

00:35:30.780 --> 00:36:00.750
<v Joel Lovell>And then I thought of the bulk of Len's body on top of his desk, the wad of bills he handed to his ex-wife, the thousands of dollars we owed that we couldn't possibly pay. I imagined that figure circled in marker, sitting on the pile in Len's office after he died. "Can you tell me?" I asked her because I genuinely wanted to know. "Can you tell me how someone else would have handled this?"
<v Joel Lovell>然后我想到了伦的身体大半压在桌子上的样子，他递给他前妻的一叠钞票，我们欠的成千上万的我们不可能偿还的钱。我想象着那个数字在马克笔下画了个圈，在他死后放在伦办公室的堆里。“你能告诉我吗？”我问她，因为我真心想知道。“你能告诉我别人会怎么处理这个吗？”

00:36:00.750 --> 00:36:07.010
<v Ira Glass>Joel Lovell, he's no longer a freelancer. Now he's the executive editor at Pineapple Street Media.
<v Ira Glass>乔尔·洛维尔，他不再是自由撰稿人了。现在他是Pineapple Street Media的执行主编。

00:36:07.010 --> 00:37:00.250
