<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - "LEN" BY COLIN MACINTYRE]

00:37:00.250 --> 00:37:10.170
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, can garbage be a force for justice? That's in a minute on Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，垃圾能成为正义的力量吗？那是我们节目在芝加哥公共电台继续时，一分钟后。

00:37:10.170 --> 00:37:24.650
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Every week on our program, of course, we choose some theme and bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, "Seemed Like a Good Idea at the Time," we've arrived at Act Three of our show. Act Three, Bad Morning America.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们的节目，当然，我们选择一个主题，带给你在这个主题上的各种不同种类的故事。今天的节目，“当时看来是个好主意”，我们已经到达了节目的第三幕。第三幕，坏的美国早晨。

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:42.740
<v Ira Glass>But every so often it actually happens-- the idea that seems so good at the time actually is. Kind of. Which brings us to Davy Rothbart's story. Davy's been on our show before. He's the creator of Found Magazine, where he publishes notes and scraps of paper and pictures just kind of randomly discovered by people on the street or wherever.
<v Ira Glass>但每隔一段时间它真的发生了——那个当时看来很好的主意确实是好的。某种程度上。这把我们带到了戴维·罗斯巴特的故事。戴维以前上过我们的节目。他是Found Magazine的创造者，他在那里出版了人们在街上或任何地方随机发现的笔记和纸条和照片。

00:37:42.740 --> 00:38:02.520
<v Ira Glass>A couple years ago, Davy was promoting a book based on the same material. He was on this book tour that went to 136 cities. And in a lot of these cities, he did the local morning TV shows. This story he's about to tell was recorded in front of a live audience. Some of the names of cities and people have been changed.
<v Ira Glass>几年前，戴维正在推销一本基于同样材料的书。他进行了这次去了136个城市的书籍巡演。而在很多这些城市，他做了当地的晨间电视节目。他要讲的这个故事是在现场观众面前录制的。一些城市和人的名字被更改了。

00:38:02.520 --> 00:38:19.650
<v Davy Rothbart>Early on in the tour, I took these gigs pretty seriously. But by the third week of the trip, I was starting to wonder who exactly, if anyone, was watching the local news at 7:00 AM. Also, while a couple of the hosts of these shows were real cool and genuinely excited about the book, most of them didn't get the whole idea behind it.
<v Davy Rothbart>在巡演早期，我相当认真地对待这些演出。但到了旅行的第三周，我开始怀疑到底是谁，如果有的话，在早上7点看当地新闻。另外，虽然这些节目的一两个主持人真的很酷，并且对书真正感到兴奋，但他们大多数人并没有理解书背后的整个想法。

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:30.160
<v Davy Rothbart>For some reason, this only increased their chipper-ness. "Those pants are so fun," they'd say, looking me up and down. "Plaid pants. You're fun, huh?"
<v Davy Rothbart>出于某种原因，这只会增加他们的快乐程度。“那些裤子真有趣，”他们会说，上下打量我。“格子裤。你很有趣，对吧？”

00:38:30.160 --> 00:39:00.340
<v Davy Rothbart>The one thing that kept me excited about these morning TV gigs was getting to meet and hang out with my other fellow guests. These were local chefs with recipes of the week, mayoral candidates, a team of Irish dancers, a kid with an 80-pound pumpkin. On Fox 45's Good Morning Baltimore I did my little song and dance, and then the anchor asked me to stay on her couch while she brought out the next guest, Baltimore's best mom, an 87-year-old woman named Darnelda Cole. She sat next to me on the couch. And on the far side of her sat her 50-year-old son Dice.
<v Davy Rothbart>让我对这些晨间电视节目感到兴奋的一件事是可以见到并和我其他同行嘉宾相处。这些是带每周食谱的当地厨师，市长候选人，一个爱尔兰舞蹈队，一个带着80磅南瓜的孩子。在福克斯45频道的《早安巴尔的摩》，我做了我的小歌舞，然后主持人要求我留在她的沙发上，当她请出下一位嘉宾时，巴尔的摩最好的妈妈，一位名叫达内尔达·科尔的87岁老妇人。她坐在沙发上我旁边。而在她远端坐着她50岁的儿子骰子。

00:39:00.340 --> 00:39:09.440
<v Davy Rothbart>Darnelda had no idea why she'd been asked to come on TV. They'd plotted this as a surprise. The anchor asked Dice Cole to read the letter he'd written. Darnelda grew weepy.
<v Davy Rothbart>达内尔达根本不知道为什么她被要求上电视。他们把这策划为一个惊喜。主持人要求骰子·科尔读他写的信。达内尔达变得泪流满面。

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:25.920
<v Davy Rothbart>At last, the anchor declared Darnelda Baltimore's Best Mom and produced an oversized plaque from somewhere and presented it to her, at which point Darnelda fell sobbing into my arms. I gave her a wild bear hug, caught up in the moment. A moment later, the anchorwoman joined our embrace.
<v Davy Rothbart>最后，主持人宣布达内尔达是巴尔的摩最好的妈妈，从某处拿出一块超大的牌匾并呈现给她，在那一点上，达内尔达啜泣着倒进了我的怀抱。我给了她一个狂野的熊抱，陷入了那一刻。片刻之后，女主持人加入了我们的拥抱。

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:45.120
<v Davy Rothbart>Dice, meanwhile, had lit up a cigarette, which an alarmed producer raced over and doused with a splash of sparkling water. Darnelda took this in and began hollering at her son and whacking him with her new plaque. "Dice, you can't smoke in here. This is TV we're making. Put that damn thing out."
<v Davy Rothbart>骰子，与此同时，点燃了一支香烟，一位惊恐的制片人跑过来，用喷洒的水浇灭了它。达内尔达接受了这一点，并开始冲着她的儿子大喊，用她的新牌匾抽他。“骰子，你不能在这里吸烟。我们正在制作电视节目。把那个该死的东西弄灭。”

00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:55.140
<v Davy Rothbart>There were other high points. And by high points, I mean low points for the stations and their guests. In Cleveland, two city parks employees showed off an injured hawk and falcon they'd rescued.
<v Davy Rothbart>还有其他高光时刻。而说高光时刻，我是说电视台及其嘉宾的低谷。在克利夫兰，两名城市公园员工展示了一只他们营救出来的受伤的老鹰和猎鹰。

00:39:55.140 --> 00:40:12.670
<v Davy Rothbart>Then the falcon got loose and started flapping about, peeing on everything. The anchors had to forge on through the local news and sports and weather while the falcon continued to dive-bomb them, rationing its urine so it could drip a few drops on them with every sortie. It was [BLEEP] amazing.
<v Davy Rothbart>然后猎鹰挣脱了，开始拍打着四处乱飞，在所有东西上撒尿。主持人们不得不在当地新闻、体育和天气中坚持下去，而猎鹰继续俯冲轰炸他们，配给它的尿液，以便它可以在每次突击时滴几滴在他们身上。这简直[消音]太神奇了。

00:40:12.670 --> 00:40:41.140
<v Davy Rothbart>In Chicago, a young soccer champ demonstrating his fancy moves booted a ball off the wall of the set, knocking it over backwards-- revealing the fact that we were not actually in the host's living room, as it might appear, but in the middle of a big, dank, concrete hangar. In Phoenix, I was sandwiched on air between Cedric the Entertainer and the governor of Arizona. Cedric came on right before me, dropped a couple F-bombs, and then sheepishly left, telling his chaperone, "I didn't mean to say that [BLEEP]. It just came out, I swear to God."
<v Davy Rothbart>在芝加哥，一位展示他花哨动作的年轻足球冠军把球踢出了布景的墙，把它向后撞倒——揭示了这样一个事实：我们实际上并不在主持人客厅，尽管看起来可能那样，而是在一个巨大的、阴冷的、混凝土机库中间。在凤凰城，我在直播中被夹在塞德里克娱乐和亚利桑那州州长中间。塞德里克在我之前出现，说了几个F开头词，然后羞怯地离开了，告诉他的护送人，“我不是故意要说那个[消音]的。它只是出来了，我向上帝发誓。”

00:40:41.140 --> 00:40:59.620
<v Davy Rothbart>I was traveling with my little brother in a van I'd bought off eBay. Often, we would do a Found event in one city, take turns driving all night to the next one, and get to the TV station parking lot around 4:00 AM. I could get a couple hours of sleep before it was time for me to unfold myself, "plomp" inside all rumpled and bleary-eyed, and do my thing for 90 seconds on air.
<v Davy Rothbart>我带着我弟弟在一个我从eBay上买的货车里旅行。通常，我们会做一场Found活动在一个城市，轮流开车整夜去下一个，并在早上4点左右到达电视台停车场。我可以睡几个小时，然后在需要我把自己展开，“啪”地一声进去，浑身皱巴巴，睡眼惺忪，在直播中做我90秒钟的事情。

00:40:59.620 --> 00:41:15.670
<v Davy Rothbart>In the wee hours, security guards in the station lots would poke flashlights in our van windows and roust us. And I'd explain that I was going to be a guest on the morning show. And they'd disappear for 20 minutes to check into it, then come back and wake us again to tell us that things had checked out and everything was cool.
<v Davy Rothbart>在凌晨时分，车站停车场的保安会用手电筒戳进我们的车窗，把我们赶起来。我会解释我要成为晨间节目的嘉宾。他们会消失20分钟去查证，然后回来再次把我们叫醒，告诉我们事情已经核实，一切都酷。

00:41:15.670 --> 00:41:32.080
<v Davy Rothbart>In Seattle, after a young security guard played this game with us, I asked him if I could come inside to use the john. We ended up talking for a while. His name was Pico. It turned out that the station was moving soon to brand-new, larger digs and that Pico was going to be replaced by an automatic gate with a swipe card.
<v Davy Rothbart>在西雅图，在一名年轻保安和我们玩了这个游戏后，我问他我能否进去使用厕所。我们最终聊了一会儿。他的名字是皮科。原来电视台很快要搬到全新的、更大的地方，而皮科要被一个带刷卡卡的自动门取代。

00:41:32.080 --> 00:41:46.090
<v Davy Rothbart>Pico asked why I was going to be on the morning show. And I explained to him the Found book, all notes and letters and photos that folks around the country had found and sent in to us, little scraps that gave a glimpse into the lives of strangers. Pico got excited.
<v Davy Rothbart>皮科问我为什么我要参加晨间节目。我向他解释了Found书，所有全国各地的人发现并寄给我们的笔记和信件和照片，揭示陌生人生活的小纸条。皮科很兴奋。

00:41:46.090 --> 00:42:04.180
<v Davy Rothbart>He told me that earlier that very same night, he'd been sifting through boxes that were being tossed out before the station's big move. And he'd found a bunch of racy notes written by the morning show's old, dour anchorman to a young camerawoman. Pico and I, we galloped out back to the dumpsters and mucked about until we found the stack of steamy pages.
<v Davy Rothbart>他告诉我，就在同一天晚上早些时候，他一直在翻看在电视台大搬迁前被扔掉的盒子。他发现了一大堆晨间节目老而严厉的主播写给一名年轻摄像师的色情笔记。皮科和我，我们奔向后面的垃圾箱，挖来挖去直到我们找到了那一叠热腾腾的页面。

00:42:04.180 --> 00:42:25.030
<v Davy Rothbart>"You should read some of these on the show," Pico cried. I thought this could be a terrible idea. But Pico was vehement. "This guy's a class-A [BLEEP]," he said. "I'm telling you. He got a janitor fired for throwing out his lucky tie that he left on the bathroom floor. She had worked here eight years."
<v Davy Rothbart>“你应该在节目上读一些这些，”皮科喊道。我以为这可能是一个糟糕的主意。但皮科很强烈。“这家伙是个一等[消音]，”他说。“我告诉你。他让一名清洁工因为扔掉了他留在浴室地板上的幸运领带而被解雇。她在那里工作了八年。”

00:42:25.030 --> 00:42:37.380
<v Davy Rothbart>Three hours later, we were on the air. And the anchorman was turning to me with a grumpy look. "So tell me about this book. You collect trash, is that it? You like trash, trashy trash? One person's treasures, another's treasure."
<v Davy Rothbart>三小时后，我们正在播出。主播带着怀疑的眼神转向我。“那么告诉我关于这本书。你收集垃圾，是这样吗？你喜欢垃圾，垃圾中的垃圾？一个人的宝贝，另一个人的宝贝。”

00:42:37.380 --> 00:43:04.240
<v Davy Rothbart>He might very well have been drunk at 7:15 in the morning. "Yes, sir," I said. "People are finding this stuff all over the country, all over the world really, and sending it in to us. It's amazing how powerfully you can get a sense of someone just from a little ripped piece of paper you pick up off the grass-- like this one, for example. Seems like it's a guy who's trying to woo a girl by describing what he'd like to do with her breasts."
<v Davy Rothbart>他很有可能在早上7点15分喝醉了。“是的，先生，”我说。“人们在全国各地发现这些东西，在全世界也是，并寄给我们。看到你仅仅从你从草地上捡起的一小片撕掉的纸，就能如此强烈地感受到某个人，这是令人惊叹的——就像这一张，例如。看起来这是一个试图通过描述他想对她的胸部做什么来追求一个女孩的家伙。”

00:43:04.240 --> 00:43:33.160
<v Davy Rothbart>I held his note up high and read it out loud. "Stacy, you've got a rack on you. Now that's a pair. I will [BLEEP] and [BLEEP] on them. Quit playing hard to get." What an expression that fellow had on his face. For a moment, his mouth, his nostrils, his eyes, and each eyebrow seemed to burst with separate looks of stunned confusion, outrage, remorse, and panic.
<v Davy Rothbart>我把他的笔记高高举起，大声朗读。“斯泰西，你有一个货架。那真是一对。我将[消音]和[消音]在它们上。放弃假装很难追。”那个家伙脸上的表情。片刻之间，他的嘴、他的鼻孔、他的眼睛和每一根眉毛似乎都迸发出惊愕的困惑、愤怒、懊悔和恐慌的各种表情。

00:43:33.160 --> 00:43:58.760
<v Davy Rothbart>Then, in an instant, he recovered-- a true professional. And he asked me, "So did you bring any other kinds of notes?" I had to admit, Pico was right-- great idea. Back in the lobby, Pico stood with two janitors by a big TV set. And as I walked out into the bright, blurry morning sun, they applauded and whistled and called after me, "Good job, man. Good job."
<v Davy Rothbart>然后，瞬间，他恢复了——一个真正的职业选手。他问我，“那么你带了任何其他种类的笔记吗？”我不得不承认，皮科是对的——伟大的主意。回到大厅，皮科和两名清洁工站在一台大电视机旁。当我走到明亮、模糊的清晨阳光下时，他们鼓掌、吹哨并向我喊，“做得好，老兄。做得好。”

00:43:58.760 --> 00:44:12.990
<v Ira Glass>Davy Rothbart, he's the creator of Found Magazine. And he writes essays and books. You can find his work at myheartisanidiotbook.com.
<v Ira Glass>戴维·罗斯巴特，他是Found Magazine的创造者。他写散文和书。你可以在myheartisanidiotbook.com找到他的作品。

00:44:12.990 --> 00:45:27.710
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "OPPORTUNITY" BY THE JEWELS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - "OPPORTUNITY" BY THE JEWELS]

00:45:27.710 --> 00:45:41.850
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Function of the Heart. When you're a kid and you're frustrated with something in your life, your strategies are kind of limited for how you can fix what's wrong. When Elspeth was a kid, she saw an opening. She got an idea.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，心脏的功能。当你是个孩子，你对生活中的某件事感到沮丧时，你关于如何解决错误的事情的策略是有限的。埃尔斯佩斯是个孩子时，她看到了一个开口。她有了个主意。

00:45:41.850 --> 00:45:55.600
<v Ira Glass>And she got results. She definitely got results. Mainly, she wanted to get closer to her dad, who was a doctor. She says what she knew about him already was mostly, well, he worked in a hospital with a lot of old men.
<v Ira Glass>她得到了结果。她绝对得到了结果。主要是，她想离她的医生爸爸更近一点。她说她关于他所知道的已经主要是，嗯，他在一家有很多老人的医院工作。

00:45:55.600 --> 00:46:12.730
<v Elspeth Carruthers>It was always a relationship where I was always just desperate for attention and for knowledge of him. I wanted to know what he was like, what he did, what he was interested in. And he was extremely un-forthcoming on every front.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>那总是这种关系，我一直渴望关注和对他的了解。我想知道他是什么样的人，他做什么，他感兴趣什么。而他在所有方面都极其不直白。

00:46:12.730 --> 00:46:42.740
<v Elspeth Carruthers>So I would ask him things like, where are you going? And he would say, out. And I would say, out where? And he would say, just out. And it would turn into that kind of incredibly frustrating word game, which he would-- he would sort of treat it as though this was playful fun. And I just remember by the end of it, I was just enraged.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>所以我总是问他类似，你要去哪儿？而他会说，出去。我会说，去哪儿？他会说，只是出去。那会变成那种极其令人沮丧的文字游戏，他会——他会某种程度上把它当作是好玩的。我只是记得到最后，我只是被激怒了。

00:46:42.740 --> 00:46:46.510
<v Ira Glass>Was he actually around? Like would he be home at night?
<v Ira Glass>他真的在吗？比如他晚上会在家吗？

00:46:46.510 --> 00:47:07.620
<v Elspeth Carruthers>Yeah. He was home. But it was important to him to have quiet. So when he'd come home, he would want quiet. And of course we were-- we would scream with enthusiasm that he would be there. And he would be clearly kind of affronted with all the noise.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>是的。他在家。但他拥有安静对他很重要。所以当他回家时，他会想要安静。当然我们是——我们会带着热情尖叫他会在那儿。他会很清楚地带着那种吵闹被冒犯。

00:47:07.620 --> 00:47:25.910
<v Elspeth Carruthers>So I was 11 sort of at the height of, I would say, desperation to get something from him. And this was a thing that was set up by the Unitarian Church. Parents would come in, and they would teach a little class in their specialty.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>所以我11岁时某种程度上处于那个高度，我会说，从他那里得到某种东西的渴望。这是一件由一神论教堂设置的事情。父母会进来，他们会教一点关于他们专业的小课。

00:47:25.910 --> 00:47:47.130
<v Elspeth Carruthers>And so my dad had volunteered. Being a doctor, he was going to give a class on the function of the heart. It was going to be on the function of the heart. And so I, of course, decided I was going to sign up for my dad's course. Because I really wanted to finally figure out what he did.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>所以我爸爸自愿参加了。作为医生，他要上一堂关于心脏功能的课。那将是关于心脏的功能。所以，当然，我决定我要报名参加我爸爸的课程。因为我真的想最终找出他做什么。

00:47:47.130 --> 00:48:07.680
<v Elspeth Carruthers>So one of my good friends, Ruthie, also signed up. So there were the two of us. And so he had gone out, and he had bought this at a butcher-- he'd gone to a butcher and brought this massive, raw, bloody cow heart in to demonstrate the function of the heart.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>所以我的一个好朋友，鲁西，也报名了。所以是我们俩。他出去了，他在肉铺买了这个——他去了肉铺带了这个巨大的、生的、血淋淋的牛心来演示心脏的功能。

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:17.160
<v Ira Glass>So the class begins and you see this bloody heart. And automatically, are you horrified to start because you thought it would be something else?
<v Ira Glass>所以课开始了，你看到了这个血淋淋的心脏。而自动地，你因为认为那会是别的东西而感到恐惧吗？

00:48:17.160 --> 00:48:36.180
<v Elspeth Carruthers>Oh, no, no. I was quite riveted. No, very cool-looking, and actually genuinely interested in the topic as well. And so he had sliced open the heart, and he opened it up. And we went through the various functions of the heart. And then he would ask questions.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>哦，不，不。我相当着迷。不，看起来很酷，而且实际上也真心对这个话题感兴趣。所以他切开了心脏，他把它打开了。我们经历了心脏的各种功能。然后他会问问题。

00:48:36.180 --> 00:49:01.280
<v Elspeth Carruthers>And there are two of us in the class. And even so, I raised my hand every time. And I don't just raise my hand. I raise my hand like, uh, uh, uh. "It's me. I know the answer. I have this. This is the question. This is the question." Yeah.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>课里只有我们两个人。即便如此，我每次都举手。我不只是举手。我举手就像，呃，呃，呃。“是我。我知道答案。我得到了这个。这是问题。这是问题。”是的。

00:49:01.280 --> 00:49:21.110
<v Elspeth Carruthers>And then he would pause. And Ruth would be sitting there quite quietly, and then he would ask what my question would be. And then I would ask the question. And then he would explain and go into a long, long explanation. And he's a very quiet man. He's a very, very quiet person, not used to speaking.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>然后他会停顿。鲁西会坐在那儿相当安静，然后他会问我的问题会是什么。然后我会问问题。然后他会解释并进入一个长长的、长长的解释。他是一个非常安静的人。他是一个非常、非常安静的人，不习惯说话。

00:49:21.110 --> 00:49:52.620
<v Elspeth Carruthers>So the explanations would go on for quite some time. And then he would ask another question. And I would again raise my hand-- and not just raise it, but pump it. I would pump my arm. And so the whole class was just me dominating the classroom over Ruthie, my very dear friend who was the soul of discretion throughout-- very subtle, kind of acute reading of the room dynamic, I would say, for an 11-year-old. Stayed right out of it.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>所以解释会持续相当长的时间。然后他会问另一个问题。我会再次举手——而且不只是举起，而是抽动它。我会抽动我的手臂。所以整个课堂只是我统治了教室，盖过了鲁西，我那个非常亲爱的朋友，她在整个过程中体现了克制——非常微妙，我会说，对于一个11岁的人来说，对房间动态的一种敏锐解读。完全没介入。

00:49:52.620 --> 00:50:04.080
<v Elspeth Carruthers>And that was the class. And after that class, I felt great. I loved it. I felt like I'd had this great class with my dad.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>而那便是课堂。在那之后，我觉得很棒。我爱它。我觉得我跟我爸爸上了这堂伟大的课。

00:50:04.080 --> 00:50:11.860
<v Ira Glass>That he saw your smart questions, and that he actually answered your questions, and gave you the attention.
<v Ira Glass>他看到了你聪明的问题，而且他实际上回答了你的问题，给了你关注。

00:50:11.860 --> 00:50:26.670
<v Elspeth Carruthers>That's right. And that I finally had some evidence about what it was that he actually did. It was a great moment. And I felt afterwards like I'd really kind of finally connected with my dad.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>没错。而且我最终得到了一些证据关于他实际上做了什么。这是一个伟大的时刻。我之后觉得好像我真的某种程度上最终跟我爸爸连接上了。

00:50:26.670 --> 00:50:41.580
<v Ira Glass>Just the poetry of it, of you in this classroom, this formal setting, with your dad-- and your best friend there for protection, basically-- and a bloody heart cut open, sitting there in between you.
<v Ira Glass>仅仅是它的诗意，你在这个教室里，这个正式的场合，和你爸爸在一起——而且你最好的朋友在那里作为保护，基本上——还有一个切开的血淋淋的心脏，坐在你们之间。

00:50:41.580 --> 00:51:10.770
<v Elspeth Carruthers>No. It's quite the tableau, yeah. But then, a couple of days later, my mother took me aside. And she sort of raised the issue about how important it was to be a quiet person. You don't want to be the loud person. This was the message. You don't want to be the loud person. You want to be the quiet person.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>不。那确实是一幅画面，是的。但后来，几天后，我母亲把我拉到一边。她某种程度上提出了关于做一个安静的人是多么重要的问题。你不想成为吵闹的人。这就是信息。你不想成为吵闹的人。你想做一个安静的人。

00:51:10.770 --> 00:51:43.980
<v Elspeth Carruthers>And by the time she's saying that, I know exactly what she's telling me. I had offended the sensibilities of my father, who would prefer that I was like Ruthie, the quiet person in the classroom. So here I thought that I was doing the right thing. And then what I learned, after the fact, was that I had been loud and vulgar somehow and that I should have just shut up and been the quiet person.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>当她说这话的时候，我知道她完全是在告诉我什么。我冒犯了我父亲的感受，他更希望我像鲁西一样，那个教室里安静的人。所以在这里我以为我正在做正确的事情。然后我学到的，事后看来，是我曾经吵闹和庸俗，某种程度上，我应该只是闭嘴做个安静的人。

00:51:43.980 --> 00:51:47.240
<v Ira Glass>So you must've been completely mortified.
<v Ira Glass>所以你一定感到完全羞愧。

00:51:47.240 --> 00:51:56.240
<v Elspeth Carruthers>I felt like a fool, I think, afterwards in some sense, that I had been so deluded, that I had gotten it so profoundly wrong.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>我觉得我像个傻瓜，我想，事后在某种意义上，我曾经如此受骗，我把它彻底弄错了。

00:51:56.240 --> 00:52:02.610
<v Ira Glass>And so was it helpful that your mom gave you this information? Or was it not so helpful in retrospect?
<v Ira Glass>所以你妈妈给你这个信息有帮助吗？还是回顾起来没那么有帮助？

00:52:02.610 --> 00:52:20.500
<v Elspeth Carruthers>It was helpful, but not in the sense that it was intended to be helpful. What my mother was telling me was to become quiet. And I just never did. And so that advice was not helpful. But knowing that I should be quiet was helpful.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>有帮助，但不是在那旨在有帮助的意义上。我母亲告诉我的是变得安静。但我从来没做过。所以那条建议没帮助。但知道我应该安静是有帮助的。

00:52:20.500 --> 00:52:47.170
<v Elspeth Carruthers>Because I knew that my dad was wrong. And I knew-- I knew-- that my behavior was completely acceptable and, in fact, was a good thing. And that was a moment when I realized, I have to kind of strategize around this relationship in a way that a child shouldn't have to. I've got to be more crafty about this in order to get through this childhood thing.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>因为我知道我爸爸是错的。而且我知道——我知道——我的行为是完全可以接受的，而且，事实上，是一件好事。那是一个时刻，我意识到，我必须某种程度上绕过这段关系制定策略，以一种孩子不应该有的方式。我必须在这方面更狡猾一点，为了熬过这个童年的事情。

00:52:47.170 --> 00:53:04.140
<v Ira Glass>I mean it's funny because the thing you're describing is this moment of a kid understanding that their parents really don't have it together all the time and are doing the wrong thing. And everybody has gone through that moment with their parents at some point. And in a way, that's an amazing thing to have happen at 11.
<v Ira Glass>我是说这很有趣因为你描述的事情是那种孩子理解他们的父母并不是总是能掌握住，并且在做错事的时刻。每个人在某一点上都经历过那个时刻与他们的父母。在某种程度上，这是一个11岁时发生的惊人的事情。

00:53:04.140 --> 00:53:26.680
<v Elspeth Carruthers>Yeah. And I did finally get to the point where I accepted the fact that I'm never going to be the quiet person. I'm always going to be an enthusiastic person who, if I have a great question, I will pump my arm. And I will continue to offend him for the duration.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>是的。我最终确实到了一个地步，我接受了这样一个事实，我永远不会成为安静的人。我总会是一个热情的、如果我有一个伟大的问题，我会抽动我的手臂的人。而且我会为了持续的时间继续冒犯他。

00:53:26.680 --> 00:53:56.870
<v Elspeth Carruthers>And then, of course, I took up the trumpet, which is the world's loudest, most vulgar instrument-- at 11, now that I think of it. So very shortly after this, I took up the trumpet. I thought, what is the loudest possible instrument I could choose? So yeah, no, I went right for the trumpet.
<v Elspeth Carruthers>然后，当然，我拿起了小号，这是世界上最吵、最庸俗的乐器——在11岁时，现在我想起来。所以在那之后不久，我拿起了小号。我想，我能选择的最吵的乐器是什么？所以是的，不，我直接奔向了小号。

00:53:56.870 --> 00:55:14.500
<v Ira Glass>Elspeth Carruthers, she's executive director for the Neubauer Collegium for Society and Culture at the University of Chicago. Since we first broadcast this story, Elspeth's dad has died. He was 83.
<v Ira Glass>埃尔斯佩斯·卡鲁瑟斯，她是芝加哥大学诺伊鲍尔社会与文化学院的执行主任。自从我们第一次广播这个故事以来，埃尔斯佩斯的爸爸已经去世了。他83岁。

